{"chunk_id": "61aff7630e90", "text": "After Arjuna (Dhananjaya) is slain in combat by his own son Vabhruvahana, Ulupi reveals that the entire event was arranged for Arjuna\u2019s ultimate good. She had brought a celestial gem capable of restoring life and instructs the prince to place it on Arjuna\u2019s chest, reviving him. When Arjuna awakens, heavenly signs\u2014flowers, celestial drums, and voices\u2014celebrate the moment. Ulupi then explains the deeper cause: Arjuna had incurred a curse from the Vasus for killing Bhishma through what they judged an unrighteous stratagem during the great war. The curse decreed that Arjuna must be cast down in battle by his own son; only then would he be freed from sin. With the curse lifted, reconciliation follows. Arjuna invites Vabhruvahana to attend Yudhishthira\u2019s upcoming horse sacrifice, but refuses to enter the city himself because he must continue following the wandering sacrificial horse in observance of his vow.\nUlupi reveals Arjuna\u2019s death by his son fulfilled a curse for Bhishma\u2019s killing; Arjuna revives, reconciles with Vabhruvahana, and continues the Ashvamedha journey.\nThemes: curse and expiation, father-son reconciliation, divine intervention, aftermath of Kurukshetra, dharma and guilt", "vector": [0.014910631813108921, -0.001380393048748374, -0.0013130359584465623, -0.0031325286254286766, -0.016220256686210632, 0.02575596794486046, -0.00605701794847846, 0.002213403582572937, -0.019125988706946373, -0.017093341797590256, 0.017884572967886925, 0.004399523138999939, 0.005886493716388941, 0.007700870744884014, -0.009276513941586018, 0.002223634859547019, 0.04179888218641281, -0.006077480968087912, 0.029984967783093452, -0.012993940152227879, -0.00498271593824029, 0.0009566405205987394, -0.007373464293777943, 0.025701399892568588, -0.015183470211923122, 0.022877519950270653, 0.004614383447915316, -0.021063143387436867, 0.009494785219430923, -1.6612782928859815e-05, -0.016588589176535606, 0.0046962350